WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.670
- If I was telling a
kid right now, I'd say,

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:04.010
brother unless, brother and sister,

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:06.700
unless there's a super
serious thing you can

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:08.500
get a degree in that you can you exchange

00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:11.150
for green rectangles, don't go.

00:00:11.150 --> 00:00:13.920
'Cause half the people going in right now

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:17.085
are getting degrees that mean nothing.

00:00:17.085 --> 00:00:19.752
(serious music)

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:27.820
- I'm Dave Rubin and this
is "The Rubin Report".

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:30.410
Reminder, everybody,
subscribe to rubinreport.com

00:00:30.410 --> 00:00:32.410
if you wanna get all our videos early

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:33.970
and totally ad-free.

00:00:33.970 --> 00:00:35.840
And more importantly, joining me today

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:39.930
is a stand up comic and host
of "Dennis Miller Plus One",

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:41.010
on the RT Network,

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:43.960
as well as the "Dennis
Miller Option" podcast.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:45.660
Dennis Miller, I've already
said your name three times.

00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:46.760
- What's up, Rubes?
- Welcome

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:48.700
to "The Rubin Report".
- Beautiful pad, here, man.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:49.533
I'm trying to read.

00:00:49.533 --> 00:00:51.120
You can tell a man by his books--

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:52.550
- How am I doing?
- And his tchotchkes.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:54.923
I see you're a Carlin fan,
which I find endearing.

00:00:54.923 --> 00:00:57.487
- Of course.
- A little Steven Pinker, NBA.

00:00:57.487 --> 00:00:59.470
I think your detractors would say

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:01.170
this indicates you're moving rightward

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.810
towards John Birch participation.

00:01:03.810 --> 00:01:04.643
on the the bottom.
(Dave laughing)

00:01:04.643 --> 00:01:06.287
Where do you have the map centered out?

00:01:06.287 --> 00:01:10.110
And that odd confluence
between South America and,

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:11.140
or the globe, I should say,

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:13.475
South America and Africa
where it's split up.

00:01:13.475 --> 00:01:14.310
And now, I have a theory.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:16.530
It looks like they were
together at one point--

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:17.990
- Yes.
- And they split apart,

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:22.440
and obviously the west coast
of Africa with Ivory Coast,

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.940
Liberia is the most contentious
place on the planet,

00:01:24.940 --> 00:01:28.290
and I think that's a
sudden hard drive tremble

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.610
from way back where a guy
went out into his yard

00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:32.900
in Liberia once and said,
Honey, what's this crack?

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:34.250
And then two weeks later--
- Next thing--

00:01:34.250 --> 00:01:36.600
- It was another continent.
- I've oft wondered

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.550
how Pangaea split.
(Dennis laughing)

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:39.840
Now I have a little--
- There you go.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:40.810
- Yeah, I'm giving you

00:01:40.810 --> 00:01:41.830
a little something of everything here,

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:43.410
'cause we got lefties, we got righties.

00:01:43.410 --> 00:01:45.194
Look, I got an American flag
in the control room over there.

00:01:45.194 --> 00:01:46.027
- And I love the map.

00:01:46.027 --> 00:01:47.950
I always love to have a map in my studio

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:49.720
to feel situated.
- Yeah.

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:53.050
So you got two shows, you've
done a bajillion things.

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:53.970
I said here right before,

00:01:53.970 --> 00:01:55.010
I'm not even gonna use notes.

00:01:55.010 --> 00:01:56.470
I try not to really look at my notes

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:58.320
when I talk to most guests anyway,

00:01:58.320 --> 00:01:59.910
but I thought I'd just sit
down and see what happens.

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:01.600
You kinda just need to be wound up

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:03.440
and then you go, right?

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:04.300
- Well, you know what?

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:06.330
But I tell you as I get older,

00:02:06.330 --> 00:02:09.200
I need to be wound up and let go less.

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:11.210
I'm just, listen, it's contentious now.

00:02:11.210 --> 00:02:13.580
I was more of a, I don't wanna use the,

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:15.250
it's a tired term, fire brin,

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:17.730
but I was more willing
to engage, lock antlers,

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:19.270
when I was young.

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:21.800
I don't think people are gonna
change their minds anymore.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:23.820
I don't wanna be the carnival barker

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:25.060
on some freak show.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:26.900
People were split.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:29.550
We'll find out where we go
each way at each election.

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:31.770
In between, it's gonna be contentious.

00:02:31.770 --> 00:02:34.550
At age 66, I don't wanna wake up every day

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:36.580
and fight with people
about stuff they're not

00:02:36.580 --> 00:02:38.320
gonna change their mind on anyway.

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.311
So when I get going, I
like to put on a good show.

00:02:41.311 --> 00:02:45.520
You're Spanky and our
gang, let's put on a show,

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:48.780
but I don't need to get
going as much anymore.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:52.460
- So you're like an evolved
political satirist or something.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:55.510
- I just, I used to dig the game

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:57.320
when you could budge somebody.

00:02:57.320 --> 00:02:58.153
An angster.

00:02:58.153 --> 00:02:59.260
I didn't need them to come over.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:00.590
I never thought that you're gonna talk

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:03.300
to somebody and they were
gonna desert their core beliefs

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.320
and come over your way.

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:07.550
But at least you could see them mulling.

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:08.730
I think mulling's gone.

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:11.010
I could see hmms gone.

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:14.360
It's just now, everybody's dug in.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:15.960
- I'm trying, man, I'm trying over here.

00:03:15.960 --> 00:03:16.793
- Nah, I appreciate it.

00:03:16.793 --> 00:03:19.890
But like I said, you're
in your 40s, right?

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:21.570
I would have probably
fought to fought more then.

00:03:21.570 --> 00:03:23.010
But I'm telling you what,

00:03:23.010 --> 00:03:25.440
you seem like just the
time we talked before,

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:26.810
you seem like a smart enough cat

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:28.980
that somewhere down the road
I can't see you fighting it

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:30.130
into the barn.

00:03:30.130 --> 00:03:32.120
Seem to be loving, a beautiful family

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:34.610
in the picture out
here, got a great place.

00:03:34.610 --> 00:03:36.740
You're kicking ass and
taking names, career wise.

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:38.180
Yeah, you should do it now.

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:40.563
But at some point, the harvest is,

00:03:42.138 --> 00:03:42.971
what are you gonna do?

00:03:42.971 --> 00:03:45.410
I'm into getting up and
reading, taking a hike,

00:03:45.410 --> 00:03:48.180
having a good meal,
appreciating the fact I married

00:03:48.180 --> 00:03:51.010
the most beautiful, witty,
charming girl I ever met,

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:53.600
adoring my sons, watching some baseball

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.520
and then reading as I fall off to sleep.

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.270
- Is that a tough thing,
though, for a comic

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:58.697
to have to face?
- No, not for me.

00:03:58.697 --> 00:04:00.470
- No, but do you think
for most comics, that's,

00:04:00.470 --> 00:04:01.441
like, you almost have to fear that?

00:04:01.441 --> 00:04:02.920
- I don't know.
- Do you fear the okayness,

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:04.660
'cause you won't have the fuel?

00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:06.660
- It's a comedian, it's a job.

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:08.523
Yeah, I was never very,

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:11.470
I don't view show business as some

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:15.090
Sword from the Stone moment
where you're anointed.

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:16.001
It's a job.

00:04:16.001 --> 00:04:16.999
I grew up in Pittsburgh.

00:04:16.999 --> 00:04:20.620
And my job, what I was good at was,

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:21.890
I would think jokes up.

00:04:21.890 --> 00:04:23.290
And then I'd try to give those jokes

00:04:23.290 --> 00:04:25.750
to somebody else to do
and I found I didn't

00:04:25.750 --> 00:04:26.850
have the ego for that.

00:04:26.850 --> 00:04:29.230
Once I saw somebody do a joke I thought,

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:31.230
up on "The Tonight Show"
and Carson laughing,

00:04:31.230 --> 00:04:32.557
I thought, "I'm not built for this,

00:04:32.557 --> 00:04:35.040
"I better become the unlikely conduit."

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:36.920
But I've never thought of myself

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:40.417
as the Silver Surfer comedian.
- Yeah.

00:04:40.417 --> 00:04:44.440
- And so no, I don't have
any moment pangs or anything,

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.640
oh, I wish I was in the frame.

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:49.150
It never was like that
when I was in the frame.

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:51.520
- Were you always political?

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:53.040
'Cause I think, I--
- No, once you get that job

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:54.760
on "SNL", you have to become political.

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:56.373
- Yeah, so weren't political before that?

00:04:56.373 --> 00:04:58.110
- I used to be a prop comic.

00:04:58.110 --> 00:04:59.640
I remember working with,

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:03.080
I was never quite Carrotopian, but I--

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:04.360
- Is that an actual word?

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:05.810
I think that's probably
in the dictionary now.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:07.271
That's depressing.

00:05:07.271 --> 00:05:09.980
- I did props when I first started,

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:12.240
'cause I thought, visually, that way.

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:13.920
But then I remember I
couldn't fit the props

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:14.753
in the overhead.

00:05:14.753 --> 00:05:15.930
I had slight props.

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:18.207
And then I thought, "I'm not
gonna wait a half an hour

00:05:18.207 --> 00:05:21.007
"at a revolving carousel

00:05:21.007 --> 00:05:22.877
"for a toilet seat--
- Right, for some inflatable.

00:05:22.877 --> 00:05:25.457
- "I could put on my head
and say alas poor turd".

00:05:26.332 --> 00:05:30.190
So I ditched out on that,
started writing jokes.

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:32.730
Like I said, sold a
couple to other comedians,

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:34.740
saw them do them and score
with them and thought,

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:36.100
"I've gotta be the guy".

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:39.693
So like I said, it was very,

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:44.010
I didn't have a whoosh,
whoosh thing about stand up.

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:46.520
I remember thinking, "Okay,
this is a good career."

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:48.250
Kind of a decision.
- So when you get

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:50.960
the "Weekend Update"
situation, the "SNL" situation,

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:52.880
then it sort of like, it was political.

00:05:52.880 --> 00:05:55.440
What year did you start at "SNL"?

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:56.900
- Somewhere in the '80s.
- Somewhere in the--

00:05:56.900 --> 00:05:58.150
- That's what I mean, though.

00:05:58.150 --> 00:05:58.983
I'm not doing that deliberately.

00:05:58.983 --> 00:06:00.420
- It's funny, yeah.
- I just,

00:06:01.450 --> 00:06:03.140
I remember I was--
- Maybe in,

00:06:03.140 --> 00:06:04.440
like, '89 or something?

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:05.273
'Cause I'm trying to remember.

00:06:05.273 --> 00:06:08.630
'Cause you on "SNL", that class was like

00:06:08.630 --> 00:06:10.680
my formative comedy year class.

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:11.513
- Yeah.
- So I was, like,

00:06:11.513 --> 00:06:13.650
12, 13, around then, and I think,

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:15.500
I think maybe like '89
or something like that.

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:16.570
- Well some people choose--
- Was it even before that?

00:06:16.570 --> 00:06:18.560
- To think of themselves
as comedic visionaries.

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:20.080
I choose to think of ourself as

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:21.830
ushering kids through puberty.

00:06:21.830 --> 00:06:24.890
We were the, if you were 12 or 13 (laughs)

00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:25.829
that's all we were.

00:06:25.829 --> 00:06:28.020
That was our 15 minutes
off from jerking off.

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:30.410
- Yeah, but you guys did it.
- "Weekend Update".

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:33.230
- See, I made it out okay.

00:06:33.230 --> 00:06:35.620
- But I remember I was in Baltimore,

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:38.983
and I had auditioned for
"SNL" maybe a month before.

00:06:40.110 --> 00:06:44.187
And then I remember they picked the cast.

00:06:44.187 --> 00:06:45.880
"USA Today" had the picture of them.

00:06:45.880 --> 00:06:47.690
I wasn't in it, I was still in Baltimore.

00:06:47.690 --> 00:06:49.400
And I was so tough at that point,

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:51.350
because on your way up
you have to be tough.

00:06:51.350 --> 00:06:54.200
You did stand up in New York 12 years.

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:55.730
I was a gladiator.

00:06:55.730 --> 00:06:56.910
And I remember seeing that picture.

00:06:56.910 --> 00:06:58.260
I didn't have a crestfallen thing.

00:06:58.260 --> 00:06:59.930
Ah, I think I came close to that.

00:06:59.930 --> 00:07:01.760
All right, keep going.

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:03.640
Then I was up doing "Letterman".

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:06.770
And somebody came into the
"Letterman" dressing room

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:09.730
and said, listen, Lorne Michaels wants

00:07:09.730 --> 00:07:12.797
to see on you eight, or no,
17, where his office was.

00:07:12.797 --> 00:07:15.300
And I said, gees, I knew something was,

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:16.890
he's not calling me down to revisit me

00:07:16.890 --> 00:07:17.723
to tell me why he didn't give it to me.

00:07:17.723 --> 00:07:19.280
- Yeah, you're really not getting it.

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:20.750
- But I'm thinking maybe I get a shot

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:22.750
on it as a comedian or something.

00:07:22.750 --> 00:07:24.100
Like Steven Wright would come on,

00:07:24.100 --> 00:07:25.530
Sam once in awhile.

00:07:25.530 --> 00:07:28.760
So I went down, I knew it
was gonna be something good,

00:07:28.760 --> 00:07:30.540
but then I walked in and I remember,

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:32.450
it's one of those moments
that stick in your head.

00:07:32.450 --> 00:07:33.800
Lorne sits at the end of the thing,

00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:36.430
he's got one of those green domed glasses,

00:07:36.430 --> 00:07:38.750
lights on his desk that gives it that

00:07:38.750 --> 00:07:40.480
Arthur Conan Doyle feel.

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:42.010
And he's got the quarter glasses on,

00:07:42.010 --> 00:07:43.860
reading scripts or something.

00:07:43.860 --> 00:07:46.120
And over his shoulder is
the Empire State Building.

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:49.940
And I remember it being,
like, red, white, and blue.

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:51.430
And it wasn't 4th of July or anything,

00:07:51.430 --> 00:07:53.545
but he looks up and he
goes, "Hey, Dennis Miller."

00:07:53.545 --> 00:07:54.378
And I go, "Hey, Lorne."

00:07:54.378 --> 00:07:56.127
And he says, "How would you
like to do 'Weekend Update'"?

00:07:56.127 --> 00:07:57.600
And I said, "I'd like that a lot."

00:07:57.600 --> 00:07:59.500
And he said, "I'll see you
tomorrow at around 11."

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:00.830
I said, "All right, sir."

00:08:00.830 --> 00:08:02.920
I walked out and I remember thinking,

00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:04.527
I'm not a big drinker,
but I remember thinking,

00:08:04.527 --> 00:08:06.747
"I'm gonna go get four
fingers of something brown

00:08:06.747 --> 00:08:08.247
"on the rocks and knock this down,

00:08:08.247 --> 00:08:10.010
"'cause this is pivotal."

00:08:10.010 --> 00:08:11.500
And that was magical.

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:14.230
I don't wanna act like I'm
sitting over here like Rasputin.

00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:15.360
I'm talking about stand up,

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:18.020
I was kinda step-by-step, Maginot line,

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:21.000
move it six inches, fall
back four, move it four.

00:08:21.000 --> 00:08:22.467
But when that happened,
I remember thinking,

00:08:22.467 --> 00:08:24.407
"All right, take some
mental Polaroids here,

00:08:24.407 --> 00:08:25.927
"don't be blase about this,

00:08:25.927 --> 00:08:28.140
"'cause that's it's
own defense mechanism."

00:08:28.140 --> 00:08:29.270
This is a big deal.

00:08:29.270 --> 00:08:30.560
Chevy Chase did this.

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:31.950
You're gonna do it now.

00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:36.110
Enjoy it 'til you either become
a success or get whacked.

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:37.850
So I did enjoy it along the way.

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:39.070
I took all those moments.

00:08:39.070 --> 00:08:41.497
And the first time you sit in that seat

00:08:41.497 --> 00:08:42.790
and they count you down.

00:08:42.790 --> 00:08:45.700
I just thought, all right, I might go out

00:08:45.700 --> 00:08:48.010
but I'm going out on my shield here.

00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:49.770
- So even though you
weren't purely political,

00:08:49.770 --> 00:08:51.910
they pegged you for that.

00:08:51.910 --> 00:08:53.610
Is that what you wanted?
- I did some topical stuff.

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:56.123
- Did you wanna do more?
- I wanted any hook.

00:08:57.420 --> 00:08:58.580
I'm not a character guy.

00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:02.170
I used to try to stay out
of the "SNL" sketches.

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:04.330
'Cause when you're with geniuses,

00:09:04.330 --> 00:09:07.685
Carvey's a comedic genius, Lovett's,

00:09:07.685 --> 00:09:11.150
sketchwise, a genius, Phil,
the ultimate clue guy.

00:09:11.150 --> 00:09:12.590
I mean the only guy, I never thought I'd

00:09:12.590 --> 00:09:14.810
see a guy like Denny, and Phil
got in the realm with him,

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:17.060
and always thought,
well, this is a serious,

00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:18.640
Mike Myers comes in.

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:21.220
So you got four leads there
who could kill the ball.

00:09:21.220 --> 00:09:22.830
I'm the fifth guy in a sketch,

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:25.000
I'm the bartender saying,
here's your gimlet,

00:09:25.000 --> 00:09:27.910
and yet, I gotta be there
blocking it for 3 1/2 hours.

00:09:27.910 --> 00:09:29.640
So I used to go around
and say to the writers

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:31.873
when they wrote me in,
I'd say, don't put me in.

00:09:31.873 --> 00:09:33.770
I wanna be like the DH.

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:36.570
I wanna pick a rock song,
swing the weighted bat

00:09:36.570 --> 00:09:39.830
down the tunnel, come out and
hit the ball for 10 minutes.

00:09:39.830 --> 00:09:41.070
- And by the end you had that, right?

00:09:41.070 --> 00:09:43.210
- Yeah, it was a groovy job.

00:09:43.210 --> 00:09:45.320
And I used to, I've probably been

00:09:45.320 --> 00:09:47.240
in around a dozen sketches over the years.

00:09:47.240 --> 00:09:48.277
And they were ones where I thought,

00:09:48.277 --> 00:09:49.110
"oh, this is good.

00:09:49.110 --> 00:09:52.227
"I should stay in this
'cause I wanna see this

00:09:52.227 --> 00:09:53.747
"when I get older."

00:09:53.747 --> 00:09:55.900
"It's a Wonderful Life", the next chapter.

00:09:55.900 --> 00:09:58.680
You knew when something
was gonna be a big hit.

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:00.360
But by and large I was really happy

00:10:00.360 --> 00:10:01.580
to have just "Weekend Update".

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:03.470
- I'm sure you've been
asked this a million times,

00:10:03.470 --> 00:10:06.310
but what do you think it is about "SNL",

00:10:06.310 --> 00:10:08.320
or the ages, or the collection,

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:10.240
or the level of talent of all the people

00:10:10.240 --> 00:10:13.343
that there's so much tragedy
around the cast members?

00:10:14.630 --> 00:10:16.070
- I'd have to back and study that.

00:10:16.070 --> 00:10:18.250
Is there?
- Well, I mean deaths,

00:10:18.250 --> 00:10:20.400
and drug use, I mean, well I guess,

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:22.810
drug use, drug use is
just Hollywood, but yeah.

00:10:22.810 --> 00:10:24.253
Yeah.
- John, Chris.

00:10:25.330 --> 00:10:27.240
I don't know, I'd like to
see the actuary tables.

00:10:27.240 --> 00:10:29.480
I always hear this thing.

00:10:29.480 --> 00:10:31.410
- Yeah, oh so you don't even
think that there's anything?

00:10:31.410 --> 00:10:33.340
- I met a lot of healthy people there.

00:10:33.340 --> 00:10:35.190
There were a few guys
who I coulda met working

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:36.990
in a hardware store where I'd know--

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:40.263
- That had it rough, too.
- Reading about him someday.

00:10:41.312 --> 00:10:42.145
(both laughing)
- All right, fair enough.

00:10:42.145 --> 00:10:42.978
I didn't think you were gonna say that.

00:10:42.978 --> 00:10:43.947
- Well, and I just say I
don't remember it that way.

00:10:43.947 --> 00:10:45.700
- Something about the
pressure of live, maybe?

00:10:45.700 --> 00:10:47.640
I don't know, something.
- You know,

00:10:47.640 --> 00:10:49.750
all that stuff seems contrived to me.

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:50.850
- Hmm.
- You're working

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:53.140
as an entertainer in some way,

00:10:53.140 --> 00:10:55.590
and all of a sudden you're on
the best entertainment vehicle

00:10:55.590 --> 00:10:57.943
for young people in the
world, quite frankly.

00:10:58.790 --> 00:11:02.387
Now, you can spend your days
sitting in a room thinking,

00:11:02.387 --> 00:11:04.576
"Oh, my stomach hurts, I'm schpilkis,

00:11:04.576 --> 00:11:06.160
"and I gotta go get loaded",

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:08.720
or you can just get on
with it and have a blast.

00:11:08.720 --> 00:11:10.590
And it is scary.

00:11:10.590 --> 00:11:14.070
And there were weeks it felt
horrible when you'd go badly.

00:11:14.070 --> 00:11:15.250
That's any gig.

00:11:15.250 --> 00:11:17.023
But that whole crash and burn thing,

00:11:17.023 --> 00:11:18.663
it's so far from that.

00:11:19.550 --> 00:11:20.770
If somebody would have said to me,

00:11:20.770 --> 00:11:22.560
at the end of the day
this is gonna cause you

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:25.403
to do yourself in, I'd have
said I'm not taking the job.

00:11:26.260 --> 00:11:28.540
It's not that magical kingdom to me.

00:11:28.540 --> 00:11:29.740
- What would you have done?

00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:31.560
If it wasn't for comedy?
- I would have liked

00:11:31.560 --> 00:11:33.280
to maybe be a shrink.
- Yeah?

00:11:33.280 --> 00:11:34.220
- I don't know.

00:11:34.220 --> 00:11:36.800
Maybe not anymore because things that used

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:38.360
to interest me about people,

00:11:38.360 --> 00:11:40.170
their psychological tales now have turned

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:41.150
into blatant whining.
(Dave laughing)

00:11:41.150 --> 00:11:43.230
So I don't think it'd be
a good shrink for today

00:11:43.230 --> 00:11:45.290
to have people come in and say,

00:11:45.290 --> 00:11:47.330
I got in a fight when
I was in fourth grade.

00:11:47.330 --> 00:11:48.770
And you go, you're fuckin' 70.

00:11:48.770 --> 00:11:49.920
Wake up.

00:11:49.920 --> 00:11:52.630
But at the time I thought
that might be interesting.

00:11:52.630 --> 00:11:53.880
What else did I wanna do?

00:11:56.930 --> 00:11:58.507
I never even thought like that.

00:11:58.507 --> 00:12:00.730
But if I said anything, I'd say a shrink.

00:12:00.730 --> 00:12:02.240
- Yeah, so what do you make of the way

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:03.450
everything's changing right now?

00:12:03.450 --> 00:12:06.070
You're doing a show on RT.

00:12:06.070 --> 00:12:07.250
You got a podcast.

00:12:07.250 --> 00:12:08.850
Everyone's got a podcast.

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:09.683
So you've shifted around.

00:12:09.683 --> 00:12:11.130
You were on cable news for years

00:12:11.130 --> 00:12:12.720
doing your thing with O'Reilly.

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:14.490
What do you make of the way
just the media landscape

00:12:14.490 --> 00:12:16.540
is changing, and how
everyone gets everything

00:12:16.540 --> 00:12:18.290
in a different way?
- Well, I'll tell you what.

00:12:18.290 --> 00:12:19.830
It might be my inner pragmatist,

00:12:19.830 --> 00:12:22.930
but I never even say
everybody's got a podcast.

00:12:22.930 --> 00:12:26.230
I'd say there's tens of thousands
of people have a podcast.

00:12:26.230 --> 00:12:28.070
You probably whittle
that down to a thousand

00:12:28.070 --> 00:12:29.667
that are even worth
getting into the business

00:12:29.667 --> 00:12:31.380
and seeing if you dig it.

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:35.420
You probably get down to 100
that really are completely

00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:36.700
in sync with the PITA principle.

00:12:36.700 --> 00:12:39.430
They're maxing it out,
whatever talent they have.

00:12:39.430 --> 00:12:42.400
You get down to around two
dozen that are killing it.

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:44.281
I think you're in that two dozen.

00:12:44.281 --> 00:12:45.610
- Thanks.
- So I don't disparage it

00:12:45.610 --> 00:12:47.340
with that everybody has a podcast.

00:12:47.340 --> 00:12:50.470
Until everybody has a podcast, no.

00:12:50.470 --> 00:12:52.933
There's a bunch of people
throwing scat up against the wall.

00:12:52.933 --> 00:12:54.940
There's a bunch of people trying it.

00:12:54.940 --> 00:12:56.360
I would try it, too, if I was young

00:12:56.360 --> 00:12:57.790
and I didn't know if it was talented.

00:12:57.790 --> 00:12:58.670
It's a great thing.

00:12:58.670 --> 00:13:01.170
It's like you used to jump
up at an open mic night,

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:02.850
why not do a podcast?

00:13:02.850 --> 00:13:05.140
But the simple fact is,
I view the attrition rate

00:13:05.140 --> 00:13:07.720
as much the same as any other
aspect of show business.

00:13:07.720 --> 00:13:08.553
- Yeah.
- I mean,

00:13:08.553 --> 00:13:10.490
look at it, brother, you're killing it.

00:13:10.490 --> 00:13:13.040
And for me to just say
everybody's got one,

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:15.110
no, you got one.
- Well, I guess,

00:13:15.110 --> 00:13:16.339
yeah, I guess, I'll take the--
- Well,

00:13:16.339 --> 00:13:17.900
that's what they think.
- I'll take the compliment.

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:19.470
Well, I guess the--
- It's math.

00:13:19.470 --> 00:13:21.820
- Right, right, so the
barrier to entry is low.

00:13:21.820 --> 00:13:23.840
But yeah, it doesn't
mean you've got something

00:13:23.840 --> 00:13:25.520
that's really cookin'.
- Barrier to entry

00:13:25.520 --> 00:13:26.790
should always be low.
- Yeah.

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:29.000
- Anybody can jump in and
try anything they want.

00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:32.360
Show business is no magic,
or I've said magic kingdom,

00:13:32.360 --> 00:13:34.840
no exalted higher plane.

00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:38.870
It's showbiz, it's you going
out to strangers and saying,

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:42.330
let me either make you
think, cry, or laugh.

00:13:42.330 --> 00:13:44.200
That's all it is at the end of the day.

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:46.440
People have always been
interested in that.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:48.210
Town criers.

00:13:48.210 --> 00:13:52.040
People, "Seventh Seal", Joey
Rod doing the puppet shows

00:13:52.040 --> 00:13:53.903
to the paupers.

00:13:55.470 --> 00:13:57.930
I don't know, to me it seems
like a hard drive issue.

00:13:57.930 --> 00:14:00.430
I'm sure there was somebody
sitting around the mud pit

00:14:00.430 --> 00:14:03.458
way back when, when in Kubricktown

00:14:03.458 --> 00:14:05.860
when somebody came in with a
femur and cracked the other,

00:14:05.860 --> 00:14:07.870
and I'm sure there was some guy trying

00:14:07.870 --> 00:14:10.790
to do a rye rejoinder to the other ape

00:14:10.790 --> 00:14:12.400
having their head crushed in. (laughs)

00:14:12.400 --> 00:14:16.260
Folks, I wanna tell you it's
been around since day one.

00:14:16.260 --> 00:14:20.040
So I don't know, I was drawn to,

00:14:20.040 --> 00:14:22.450
to me, it should be an
easy point of entry.

00:14:22.450 --> 00:14:23.960
It shouldn't be that high a bar.

00:14:23.960 --> 00:14:25.730
It's just you trying to entertain people.

00:14:25.730 --> 00:14:28.200
- Yeah, so what really
interests you these days?

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:29.033
Like, what's really exciting?
- I'm trying

00:14:29.033 --> 00:14:31.863
to read everything PG Wodehouse ever read.

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:33.593
I hike.

00:14:35.350 --> 00:14:38.683
Let's see, I'm trying
to teach myself to not,

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:44.070
active fame's pretty, it
comes at you fast and furious.

00:14:44.070 --> 00:14:45.507
Speaking of Kubrick, it reminds me

00:14:45.507 --> 00:14:48.440
of Keir Dullea at the end
of "2001" where it's just.

00:14:48.440 --> 00:14:50.410
So you can't always be--
- That's what the whole world

00:14:50.410 --> 00:14:51.470
feels like right now, doesn't it?

00:14:51.470 --> 00:14:53.590
- Yeah, you're in a chute,
man, a wind tunnel test,

00:14:53.590 --> 00:14:54.423
and it's flying.
- Yeah.

00:14:54.423 --> 00:14:56.520
- So you can't always be lucid about it.

00:14:56.520 --> 00:15:01.520
I think that if you're on
the planet for 85 years,

00:15:02.350 --> 00:15:04.094
the front end of that 20,

00:15:04.094 --> 00:15:05.420
and there's guys like Eddie who beat it,

00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:07.610
but usually 'til 20 you're anonymous.

00:15:07.610 --> 00:15:10.600
And the last 10 years you're probably,

00:15:10.600 --> 00:15:13.240
so 30 of those out, you
got a half a century.

00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:16.530
If you get any part of
that where you're famous

00:15:16.530 --> 00:15:19.030
and you're actually
seated at a nice table,

00:15:19.030 --> 00:15:22.272
where strangers come up to
you and say, you've won.

00:15:22.272 --> 00:15:24.500
(Dave laughing)
And to try to think,

00:15:24.500 --> 00:15:27.060
this is the way life should be, it isn't.

00:15:27.060 --> 00:15:29.430
So when you say, what are you doing,

00:15:29.430 --> 00:15:31.370
I'm trying to, what do they call it?

00:15:31.370 --> 00:15:32.203
Mindfulness.

00:15:32.203 --> 00:15:36.270
But that's almost falling
into its own cliche.

00:15:36.270 --> 00:15:39.930
I'm trying to be aware of
what a great run I had,

00:15:39.930 --> 00:15:42.120
and I might be in the (speaking
foreign language) of that,

00:15:42.120 --> 00:15:43.080
and that's fine.

00:15:43.080 --> 00:15:45.760
But you can see when you
even mention it to people

00:15:45.760 --> 00:15:47.850
where you say, I don't
think I was hot as I was.

00:15:47.850 --> 00:15:49.980
I don't know if I'll
ever be that hot again,

00:15:49.980 --> 00:15:52.600
people kinda wince and go, oh, I think.

00:15:52.600 --> 00:15:54.260
I mean it sincerely.

00:15:54.260 --> 00:15:57.590
I think the examined life is everything.

00:15:57.590 --> 00:16:00.230
And I wanna examine my life.

00:16:00.230 --> 00:16:05.150
I wanna feel legit here as I,
hopefully another 30 years,

00:16:05.150 --> 00:16:06.640
get it into the barn,

00:16:06.640 --> 00:16:08.500
I wanna analyze what part of me,

00:16:08.500 --> 00:16:12.120
which was a shy kid, ends up
getting braggadocios enough

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:15.170
to try showbiz, hits the ball hard,

00:16:15.170 --> 00:16:19.360
and I think I inhabited
that plane pretty well.

00:16:19.360 --> 00:16:20.800
And now, not as hot,

00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:23.120
I wanna inhabit that plane just as well.

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:25.220
It's equally valid.

00:16:25.220 --> 00:16:26.490
I--
- I love the answer.

00:16:26.490 --> 00:16:28.122
I mean, that's as honest
as it can get, I think.

00:16:28.122 --> 00:16:29.333
- Well, I like that.

00:16:29.333 --> 00:16:32.650
I never predicated on me riding

00:16:32.650 --> 00:16:35.223
into the sunset with the key light on me.

00:16:36.280 --> 00:16:37.700
Listen, you have your moment.

00:16:37.700 --> 00:16:38.993
It's hard to, I don't know,

00:16:38.993 --> 00:16:41.360
I don't know far you're into it now,

00:16:41.360 --> 00:16:43.120
you told me you did 12 years of stand up,

00:16:43.120 --> 00:16:45.280
so I know you've got some
hard riding under your belt.

00:16:45.280 --> 00:16:46.330
- I stood on a lot of street corners.

00:16:46.330 --> 00:16:47.600
- And you're killing it now.

00:16:47.600 --> 00:16:51.480
But I don't know that humans are built

00:16:51.480 --> 00:16:52.890
to keep it up, 'til they're,

00:16:52.890 --> 00:16:54.390
all of a sudden you're Larry Sanders

00:16:54.390 --> 00:16:55.770
doing the show at seven and going,

00:16:55.770 --> 00:16:57.659
I don't mean Gary, I mean--
- Yeah, no, you mean Larry.

00:16:57.659 --> 00:16:58.660
- Yeah, I mean Larry Sanders going back

00:16:58.660 --> 00:17:00.460
and laying in bed and watching the repeat.

00:17:00.460 --> 00:17:03.200
That four hours between live show

00:17:03.200 --> 00:17:05.280
and watching it on the East Coast feed,

00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:07.023
there's just no life.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:17:07.023 --> 00:17:08.770
I think about it sometimes,

00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:10.520
even though I'm sorta right
in the thick of it right now.

00:17:10.520 --> 00:17:13.440
It's like when things change, or I change,

00:17:13.440 --> 00:17:16.640
or family expands, or
whatever it is that, like,

00:17:16.640 --> 00:17:18.320
will I wanna do it at this level,

00:17:18.320 --> 00:17:20.040
or you deal with a certain amount of hate,

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:22.170
or any of those things, you know?

00:17:22.170 --> 00:17:23.871
- Well, you'll only find out--
- There's only one way

00:17:23.871 --> 00:17:24.710
to find out.
- I don't wanna sound like I'm

00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:26.500
on "The Bachelor" on your trip,

00:17:26.500 --> 00:17:29.330
but all I know is it was never my trip

00:17:29.330 --> 00:17:30.480
to take it all the way.

00:17:31.411 --> 00:17:33.800
And at some point I remember thinking,

00:17:33.800 --> 00:17:35.650
it's like base camps on Everest.

00:17:35.650 --> 00:17:37.650
And all of a sudden
you're planting a flag,

00:17:37.650 --> 00:17:39.300
you're thinking, "How the
hell did this happen?"

00:17:39.300 --> 00:17:41.640
And then all of a sudden
when you're at six base camp

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:44.360
and you're boiling water with the Sherpa,

00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:47.620
they say, we're not gonna let
you make the climb tomorrow,

00:17:47.620 --> 00:17:49.760
you go, okay, I'm gonna get cozy in here

00:17:49.760 --> 00:17:52.210
with Tenzig Norgay the fourth. (laughs)

00:17:52.210 --> 00:17:53.250
I'm fine with that.

00:17:53.250 --> 00:17:56.200
They start moving me down the
mountain, I had a big run.

00:17:56.200 --> 00:17:59.070
- Do you think most people
are afraid of that, sort of?

00:17:59.070 --> 00:18:01.510
And that is why everyone is kinda nuts?

00:18:01.510 --> 00:18:03.960
Like, we're all sort of afraid of trying

00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:06.623
to figure out what would
make us actually happy?

00:18:07.680 --> 00:18:10.410
So we get obsessed with
whatever the news cycle is,

00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:11.281
or whatever else?

00:18:11.281 --> 00:18:12.114
- I don't wanna give you pat answer,

00:18:12.114 --> 00:18:13.830
then I turn into the
guy in the second row--

00:18:13.830 --> 00:18:15.380
- Don't be that guy.
- At a Tony Robbins thing.

00:18:15.380 --> 00:18:19.043
I'm not as scared shitless
of anonymity as some people.

00:18:20.331 --> 00:18:22.290
If I fall back into that,

00:18:22.290 --> 00:18:26.060
I'm gonna examine that in the
same way I examined being,

00:18:26.060 --> 00:18:26.893
I don't know,

00:18:26.893 --> 00:18:28.070
I remember the first two
years of being famous.

00:18:28.070 --> 00:18:29.380
Maybe this was a tell.

00:18:29.380 --> 00:18:31.340
And when I say famous,
folks, where's the camera?

00:18:31.340 --> 00:18:32.790
I don't wanna sound like an asshole.

00:18:32.790 --> 00:18:34.680
I'm just saying you end up famous.

00:18:34.680 --> 00:18:36.530
I remember spending two years up front

00:18:36.530 --> 00:18:38.330
not even being able to focus on it

00:18:38.330 --> 00:18:42.970
because I would notice it, I
would be too pleased with it,

00:18:42.970 --> 00:18:45.530
the pragmatic side of myself
would castigate the side

00:18:45.530 --> 00:18:47.780
that was too pleased with it,

00:18:47.780 --> 00:18:49.520
the other side would come in and defend me

00:18:49.520 --> 00:18:50.650
against the mean side.

00:18:50.650 --> 00:18:52.140
Say, okay, come on, he's just got,

00:18:52.140 --> 00:18:55.800
and I'd say, I'm spending 12
hours a day figuring this out.

00:18:55.800 --> 00:18:56.853
And at some point I remember,

00:18:56.853 --> 00:18:59.660
it's like an algebraic
equation, cancel terms out.

00:18:59.660 --> 00:19:00.840
It just is.

00:19:00.840 --> 00:19:03.230
It doesn't mean anything, in a weird way.

00:19:03.230 --> 00:19:04.970
Are you a good dad?

00:19:04.970 --> 00:19:06.223
Are you a good friend?

00:19:07.221 --> 00:19:09.290
And I'm not saying I am.

00:19:09.290 --> 00:19:11.180
As far as friends, I'm a little distant.

00:19:11.180 --> 00:19:13.680
But I'm just saying there are so many ways

00:19:13.680 --> 00:19:15.680
to shoot your life through.

00:19:15.680 --> 00:19:17.970
And when you're in it, it
takes all your attention,

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:19.570
'cause you gotta stay laser focused.

00:19:19.570 --> 00:19:21.550
I always view fame like it's a party.

00:19:21.550 --> 00:19:23.010
All of a sudden you're outside

00:19:23.010 --> 00:19:26.040
and it's like those old
Warner Brothers cartoons

00:19:26.040 --> 00:19:27.857
where the house has got notes coming out.

00:19:27.857 --> 00:19:29.400
♪ Da, da, da, da, na, na, na ♪

00:19:29.400 --> 00:19:30.670
All of a sudden you get in the door

00:19:30.670 --> 00:19:32.250
and you, like, stay against the wall

00:19:32.250 --> 00:19:33.550
and it's a Saurti's caricature.

00:19:33.550 --> 00:19:35.610
Every, it's like a last supper of fame

00:19:35.610 --> 00:19:37.290
and you're pressed against the wall

00:19:37.290 --> 00:19:39.140
looking at it, like, holy shit.

00:19:39.140 --> 00:19:41.870
You edge into the party,
and then all of a sudden,

00:19:41.870 --> 00:19:44.340
the sweet spot is halfway into the party.

00:19:44.340 --> 00:19:46.050
And then you, like, have two seconds there

00:19:46.050 --> 00:19:48.557
and then you realize half the
party's happening behind you

00:19:48.557 --> 00:19:52.380
and you don't know who's gonna
"Y tu Brute" in the sauna.

00:19:52.380 --> 00:19:54.410
And eventually you get
through to the other wall

00:19:54.410 --> 00:19:55.770
and go to the bathroom.

00:19:55.770 --> 00:19:59.180
So at some point I always
had that in my head

00:19:59.180 --> 00:20:01.050
from the first moment that I got it.

00:20:01.050 --> 00:20:02.790
And so I'm not disappointed.

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:05.220
It always seemed to have a
natural shelf life to me.

00:20:05.220 --> 00:20:07.490
- Yeah, so I don't wanna do
the greatest hits with you

00:20:07.490 --> 00:20:10.410
'cause I wanna do sorta
where you're at now.

00:20:10.410 --> 00:20:13.270
But so when you, then,
start doing "O'Reilly",

00:20:13.270 --> 00:20:14.640
and then suddenly people are going,

00:20:14.640 --> 00:20:17.630
wait a minute, this is the
"SNL Weekend Update" guy.

00:20:17.630 --> 00:20:20.860
He's like this sort of,
like, hip, cool, lib kinda.

00:20:20.860 --> 00:20:23.170
Suddenly you're on "O'Reilly".
- I was as hip or cool,

00:20:23.170 --> 00:20:25.720
or as not hip and cool, I was the same.

00:20:25.720 --> 00:20:28.160
- But the optics of it.

00:20:28.160 --> 00:20:29.960
Now, suddenly, wait a minute,
this doesn't make sense.

00:20:29.960 --> 00:20:32.705
He was on "SNL" and now
he's on evil Fox News.

00:20:32.705 --> 00:20:34.420
This is not my opinion,
this is just sorta like--

00:20:34.420 --> 00:20:36.710
- Yeah, well, people have to
figure their own thing out.

00:20:36.710 --> 00:20:38.500
- Yeah, but did you fear that at all?

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:40.370
Or even, none of it?
- No, no.

00:20:40.370 --> 00:20:41.203
- Interesting.

00:20:41.203 --> 00:20:44.090
- Listen, man, I was on
the number one rated show

00:20:44.090 --> 00:20:47.690
on cable, I think, Bill for 20 years.

00:20:47.690 --> 00:20:49.660
I was doing six minutes a week.

00:20:49.660 --> 00:20:50.820
It was a beautiful gig.

00:20:50.820 --> 00:20:52.250
We'd go out on the road once a week,

00:20:52.250 --> 00:20:55.180
or once a month, we'd sell
10,000 seats. (laughs)

00:20:55.180 --> 00:20:56.280
- Crazy.

00:20:56.280 --> 00:20:57.113
Could you believe that?

00:20:57.113 --> 00:20:58.490
Could he believe it?

00:20:58.490 --> 00:21:02.330
- We definitely were a one
plus one equals three thing.

00:21:02.330 --> 00:21:05.620
'Cause on my own I'd probably,
at that point draw two.

00:21:05.620 --> 00:21:08.030
I don't know what Billy was
drawing, but probably two.

00:21:08.030 --> 00:21:10.470
But you put us together and
the Mutt and Jeff of it,

00:21:10.470 --> 00:21:11.770
it always reminded me of that scene

00:21:11.770 --> 00:21:14.670
in the Chaplin movie, not
saying we were Chaplinesque,

00:21:14.670 --> 00:21:17.070
where Charlie puts his foot behind his leg

00:21:17.070 --> 00:21:20.050
and he kicks the guy who's
working the border guard thing,

00:21:20.050 --> 00:21:23.590
or the guy at Ellis Island
kicks him in the butt.

00:21:23.590 --> 00:21:25.300
I was doing that to O'Reilly.

00:21:25.300 --> 00:21:27.840
And the key moment in
that relationship was

00:21:27.840 --> 00:21:31.390
when I told Bill, you're
like a big Mick beat cop,

00:21:31.390 --> 00:21:33.950
Irish beat cop, where
you're spinning that stick,

00:21:33.950 --> 00:21:35.780
everybody's scared shitless of you,

00:21:35.780 --> 00:21:38.500
and I'm gonna call you
Billy instead of Bill.

00:21:38.500 --> 00:21:40.900
That why was a great move.

00:21:40.900 --> 00:21:44.280
Because all of a sudden the power,

00:21:44.280 --> 00:21:49.250
the monolith is being poked fun
at by the Mutt and Jeff guy.

00:21:49.250 --> 00:21:51.000
And it was cool.

00:21:51.000 --> 00:21:53.100
That's why it worked together is 'cause

00:21:53.100 --> 00:21:57.640
I could make him laugh, an
unguarded laugh periodically,

00:21:57.640 --> 00:22:00.590
and I could poke fun at
him, I could call him Billy,

00:22:00.590 --> 00:22:02.150
I could wear shorts on his show,

00:22:02.150 --> 00:22:05.190
which I'd put my leg up and
he'd say, do you not have pants?

00:22:05.190 --> 00:22:06.550
All that worked.

00:22:06.550 --> 00:22:07.550
And that's why--
- Were you doing

00:22:07.550 --> 00:22:08.740
a lot of those hits from home?

00:22:08.740 --> 00:22:10.260
Did you have a home
situation, or were you going

00:22:10.260 --> 00:22:12.090
into Fox affiliators often?
- No.

00:22:12.090 --> 00:22:14.090
I went over to this place
called the Rubin Studios.

00:22:14.090 --> 00:22:18.870
No, I, we did it up in Santa
Barbara in another studio.

00:22:18.870 --> 00:22:20.430
We were always on a split screen.

00:22:20.430 --> 00:22:22.440
It was so funny, until we
started going on the road

00:22:22.440 --> 00:22:24.840
people would say, you and Bill,
like we're Hope and Crosby.

00:22:24.840 --> 00:22:26.800
I said, I've met him twice. (laughs)

00:22:26.800 --> 00:22:28.480
We're always on split screen.

00:22:28.480 --> 00:22:29.850
But over the years, I'll tell you what.

00:22:29.850 --> 00:22:33.383
I'm relatively ill-at-ease socially.

00:22:35.560 --> 00:22:37.740
I don't know what that is, I
don't wanna make it precious.

00:22:37.740 --> 00:22:40.010
Bill, I think if you could
watch him over the years,

00:22:40.010 --> 00:22:40.910
he's a little--
- Are you?

00:22:40.910 --> 00:22:42.737
'Cause off camera you seem
exactly the same to me--

00:22:42.737 --> 00:22:44.280
- Well--
- Where, no, but there's

00:22:44.280 --> 00:22:45.343
plenty of comics, I know exactly
what you're talking about.

00:22:45.343 --> 00:22:47.640
There's a lot of comics that are weird,

00:22:47.640 --> 00:22:49.860
have eye contact stuff,
don't wanna touch anybody,

00:22:49.860 --> 00:22:51.600
and then the second the camera goes on,

00:22:51.600 --> 00:22:52.540
and bam, they're there.

00:22:52.540 --> 00:22:55.330
But from the little bit
we just did our thing,

00:22:55.330 --> 00:22:56.497
I didn't catch that.
- Well, I do know

00:22:56.497 --> 00:22:59.060
that I don't go to a lot of social events

00:22:59.060 --> 00:23:01.070
and stuff like that when I'm working.

00:23:01.070 --> 00:23:02.797
I meet a Hale fellow while I'm at,

00:23:02.797 --> 00:23:06.820
I like to say hi and be
normal, I'm in a cat's place.

00:23:06.820 --> 00:23:09.490
But I'm not a real social
guy and he isn't either.

00:23:09.490 --> 00:23:11.933
So when you put the two of us together,

00:23:11.933 --> 00:23:14.880
I was enamored at that part of O'Reilly.

00:23:14.880 --> 00:23:17.680
And I sometimes didn't
understand his brusk manner,

00:23:17.680 --> 00:23:20.077
and I something thought,
"Why is he beating his drum

00:23:20.077 --> 00:23:22.120
"to that degree", when
I'd watch him on the show.

00:23:22.120 --> 00:23:23.940
And Bill, if you're seeing this,

00:23:23.940 --> 00:23:27.430
I'm not, you know, I'm telling you,

00:23:27.430 --> 00:23:31.120
I saw that cat do stuff for soldiers,

00:23:31.120 --> 00:23:36.120
kids who'd been abused,
Haitians after storms,

00:23:36.780 --> 00:23:41.440
yeah, sometimes the, in the
way that doesn't matter,

00:23:41.440 --> 00:23:45.350
the topical stuff, I sometimes
found him equally ill-at-ease

00:23:45.350 --> 00:23:48.860
as I am, but walking the walk,

00:23:48.860 --> 00:23:52.130
that cat gave a lot of
money to a lot of people.

00:23:52.130 --> 00:23:53.870
And I don't know his personal life.

00:23:53.870 --> 00:23:54.790
People say, what about?

00:23:54.790 --> 00:23:55.970
I go, do you really think?

00:23:55.970 --> 00:23:58.940
Oh, okay, so that's my
relationship with O'Reilly.

00:23:58.940 --> 00:24:00.450
I'm gonna call him and talk about dating,

00:24:00.450 --> 00:24:02.150
or whatever the hell's going on.

00:24:02.150 --> 00:24:03.490
It's not my life with him.

00:24:03.490 --> 00:24:05.650
We went out on the road and I saw him

00:24:05.650 --> 00:24:07.870
do a lot of good things
for a lot of people.

00:24:07.870 --> 00:24:08.750
And guess what?

00:24:08.750 --> 00:24:11.330
I felt no need to know,
to probe the underbelly

00:24:11.330 --> 00:24:14.340
of Bill O'Reilly, or find out
what really makes him tick,

00:24:14.340 --> 00:24:16.080
and he felt no need to find that.

00:24:16.080 --> 00:24:18.990
That being said, I did
see him do enough things

00:24:18.990 --> 00:24:21.080
over the years that I
find him an honorable man.

00:24:21.080 --> 00:24:22.423
- Yeah, what do you think about the way

00:24:22.423 --> 00:24:24.290
that we take out people these days?

00:24:24.290 --> 00:24:25.640
You don't have to make it
about him, specifically,

00:24:25.640 --> 00:24:26.740
but just generally.

00:24:26.740 --> 00:24:28.010
Now, Chris Matthews went out,

00:24:28.010 --> 00:24:29.670
and it's just like this endless cascade

00:24:29.670 --> 00:24:31.730
of we're just gonna build
people up and crush 'em.

00:24:31.730 --> 00:24:32.563
- It gets worse.

00:24:33.440 --> 00:24:36.140
Listen, imagine, like, Twitter

00:24:36.140 --> 00:24:37.940
during DeKinsey in England.
(Dave laughing)

00:24:37.940 --> 00:24:39.740
Imagine how cruel it would be there?

00:24:40.590 --> 00:24:42.970
Listen, the rules are clear--
- You mean, it's not worse

00:24:42.970 --> 00:24:43.803
than it's ever been?

00:24:43.803 --> 00:24:44.740
That's what everyone says every day.

00:24:44.740 --> 00:24:46.090
It's worse than it's ever been.

00:24:46.090 --> 00:24:47.800
- I think the way that it'll turn this,

00:24:47.800 --> 00:24:50.180
yeah, it might be, but I think
the way that'll turn this is

00:24:50.180 --> 00:24:52.810
everybody who's in the
public eye should get up

00:24:52.810 --> 00:24:57.240
to a joke, a witticism,
a pithy revelation,

00:24:57.240 --> 00:25:01.140
and stop it down themselves
and look at the camera and say,

00:25:01.140 --> 00:25:03.810
I have something here,
but I'm going to stay out

00:25:03.810 --> 00:25:05.550
because I understand the new rules.

00:25:05.550 --> 00:25:08.853
And have that happen ad
nauseum for a few years.

00:25:08.853 --> 00:25:12.820
And I relate to people how
crazy and/or post-Orwellian

00:25:12.820 --> 00:25:17.300
it's gotten as far as jokes,
dialogue, throwaways, cruelty.

00:25:17.300 --> 00:25:20.170
Indeed, humans are cruel once in awhile.

00:25:20.170 --> 00:25:22.900
The fact that that's
all neutered down now,

00:25:22.900 --> 00:25:24.590
I think people should be reminded

00:25:24.590 --> 00:25:26.850
that we're missing some
of the texture of life,

00:25:26.850 --> 00:25:29.743
walking around like we're in
"Logan's Run" or something.

00:25:31.020 --> 00:25:33.070
I don't think it will change for awhile,

00:25:33.070 --> 00:25:35.610
because to me, it's the
"Lord of the Flies",

00:25:35.610 --> 00:25:38.400
and right now, the uptight
people have the conch,

00:25:38.400 --> 00:25:39.890
and they're the ones who are gonna speak.

00:25:39.890 --> 00:25:41.870
And therefore, if I'm
sitting there and I get

00:25:41.870 --> 00:25:44.230
to a point in the joke where I go,

00:25:44.230 --> 00:25:46.740
this puts me where I
have to talk to people

00:25:46.740 --> 00:25:49.350
who I disagree with about how bad I am,

00:25:49.350 --> 00:25:53.970
I stop often and go, nah,
I'm not gonna say it.

00:25:53.970 --> 00:25:55.500
I've tackled myself here.

00:25:55.500 --> 00:25:57.180
And if enough of that happens,

00:25:57.180 --> 00:25:59.330
people are gonna start to
say, hey, I'm sick of this.

00:25:59.330 --> 00:26:01.330
- Yeah, do you have a bunch that you just

00:26:01.330 --> 00:26:02.410
wouldn't tell anymore?
- Yeah, sure.

00:26:02.410 --> 00:26:04.030
- Like an old favorite
that you're just like--

00:26:04.030 --> 00:26:05.810
- Yeah, it's easy.

00:26:05.810 --> 00:26:07.530
Listen, when I used to
be on network television,

00:26:07.530 --> 00:26:08.940
they told you not to swear.

00:26:08.940 --> 00:26:10.860
I found it easy not to swear.

00:26:10.860 --> 00:26:13.260
When I'm in Vegas, I
found it easy to say fuck.

00:26:13.260 --> 00:26:15.110
When I'm here, I don't know
what the rules are here,

00:26:15.110 --> 00:26:16.740
you'll bleep 'em--
- No, we don't bleep.

00:26:16.740 --> 00:26:17.820
- Yeah, it's easy.
- What do you wanna say?

00:26:17.820 --> 00:26:18.653
Come on.
- No, I don't even want to.

00:26:18.653 --> 00:26:20.190
- Let's get you canceled
right now, man, come on.

00:26:20.190 --> 00:26:21.270
- I don't even think about it.

00:26:21.270 --> 00:26:26.060
But I am saying that yes,

00:26:26.060 --> 00:26:29.260
and not so much this
point and oh my career

00:26:29.260 --> 00:26:31.030
will be taken, I don't care about that.

00:26:31.030 --> 00:26:34.200
I care about having to spend time reacting

00:26:34.200 --> 00:26:36.560
to nimrods who, quite frankly,

00:26:36.560 --> 00:26:38.760
think second guessing is creativity.

00:26:38.760 --> 00:26:39.860
It isn't.
- I love that.

00:26:39.860 --> 00:26:41.620
- I like being with creative people.

00:26:41.620 --> 00:26:44.699
I like being with people
who say things occasion,

00:26:44.699 --> 00:26:46.960
(laughs) I can't believe you said that.

00:26:46.960 --> 00:26:50.383
And I don't think it's over then.

00:26:51.670 --> 00:26:55.290
Listen, the human fray is cacophonous.

00:26:55.290 --> 00:26:58.550
It's like, where was it
written that you're never

00:26:58.550 --> 00:27:00.963
gonna hear anything that falls on your,

00:27:02.112 --> 00:27:04.562
the aphelia in your ear
and you'll disagree with.

00:27:05.460 --> 00:27:07.370
I don't wanna lead that life.

00:27:07.370 --> 00:27:09.867
So well, therefore, I'll stop
myself just because I think,

00:27:09.867 --> 00:27:11.907
"Christ, who needs the bring down

00:27:11.907 --> 00:27:15.000
"of some whiny kid telling
me how I've ruined his day?"

00:27:15.000 --> 00:27:17.690
- Yeah, I used to do, there's some,

00:27:17.690 --> 00:27:19.070
my old stand up tapes are right there.

00:27:19.070 --> 00:27:20.680
And I used to do a joke way back when

00:27:20.680 --> 00:27:21.780
that I used to say the n-word.

00:27:21.780 --> 00:27:23.970
And it was not to be racist in any way.

00:27:23.970 --> 00:27:26.880
It was actually a stupid,
silly "Transformers" reference

00:27:26.880 --> 00:27:29.745
that I was imitating a
Transformer and I would say this.

00:27:29.745 --> 00:27:31.870
And it would always get
a huge laugh, huge laugh.

00:27:31.870 --> 00:27:33.090
I would close with it sometimes.

00:27:33.090 --> 00:27:35.870
And then one night I was
onstage at Gotham Comedy Club

00:27:35.870 --> 00:27:38.910
on 24th, and the audience
kinda turned on me.

00:27:38.910 --> 00:27:42.310
This is like, maybe 2004,
something like that.

00:27:42.310 --> 00:27:44.340
And I remember thinking,
"I'm putting that one away".

00:27:44.340 --> 00:27:45.800
And I never did it again.

00:27:45.800 --> 00:27:48.290
I never liked that feeling,
but I was just like--

00:27:48.290 --> 00:27:49.327
- Yeah, some people wanna--
- So this is way

00:27:49.327 --> 00:27:50.810
before all that.
- Do a Nantucket sleigh ride

00:27:50.810 --> 00:27:52.660
with a bit where they ride at the bottom?

00:27:52.660 --> 00:27:54.230
- Yeah.
- It doesn't interest me.

00:27:54.230 --> 00:27:55.880
- Yeah.
- I'm out to please people.

00:27:55.880 --> 00:27:57.110
I like to get laughs.

00:27:57.110 --> 00:27:59.350
And at some point, if I have an opinion

00:27:59.350 --> 00:28:02.630
and I feel it's worth it, I'll push it in,

00:28:02.630 --> 00:28:04.100
even if it draws ire.

00:28:04.100 --> 00:28:06.160
But there are others times I
think this isn't good enough

00:28:06.160 --> 00:28:08.930
to let all this agita into my life.

00:28:08.930 --> 00:28:11.090
The Sturm and Drang's tiresome.

00:28:11.090 --> 00:28:13.207
It's so precious to me
that we sit down every day,

00:28:13.207 --> 00:28:15.110
and 10 times a day somebody has

00:28:15.110 --> 00:28:18.770
to get themselves back and supine.

00:28:18.770 --> 00:28:20.810
And I remember when, years ago,

00:28:20.810 --> 00:28:23.340
there was a cat who was the general manger

00:28:23.340 --> 00:28:25.760
for the Los Angeles Dodgers, Al Campanis.

00:28:25.760 --> 00:28:26.593
Do you remember him?
- Yeah, yeah, oh yeah.

00:28:26.593 --> 00:28:28.785
- He goes on Ted--
- He was before I was here,

00:28:28.785 --> 00:28:29.618
but yeah, I remember him.
- He goes

00:28:29.618 --> 00:28:32.640
on "Ted Koppel" one night
and he says something that's,

00:28:32.640 --> 00:28:34.653
like, from another generation, racially.

00:28:35.860 --> 00:28:37.060
And he's dead.

00:28:37.060 --> 00:28:37.893
He's dead.

00:28:37.893 --> 00:28:41.729
He's gotta go supplicate for Sharpton,

00:28:41.729 --> 00:28:44.623
all that silly game.

00:28:46.402 --> 00:28:47.780
- 'Cause Sharpton's so righteous

00:28:47.780 --> 00:28:49.119
- Yeah.

00:28:49.119 --> 00:28:51.190
And I remember thinking,
when Jackie Robinson

00:28:51.190 --> 00:28:53.990
first came up, and he goes
to Montreal and the Dodgers,

00:28:53.990 --> 00:28:55.500
and he's playing minor league baseball,

00:28:55.500 --> 00:28:58.220
and they gotta sign somebody
to go out with the cat

00:28:58.220 --> 00:29:03.010
and ride wing man, I think
Campanis is right there.

00:29:03.010 --> 00:29:06.100
I mean, when it came to
real life he's there.

00:29:06.100 --> 00:29:08.020
And then later he says something brutish

00:29:08.020 --> 00:29:10.940
from the day gone by, and he's over.

00:29:10.940 --> 00:29:14.840
And I remember thinking,
"Oh, this is so tedious".

00:29:14.840 --> 00:29:17.030
That's the smartest thing
Eric Holder ever said,

00:29:17.030 --> 00:29:18.930
although I think he back ended into it.

00:29:18.930 --> 00:29:21.110
I don't even think he
meant it the way I took it

00:29:21.110 --> 00:29:22.197
when he said, "We're cowards.

00:29:22.197 --> 00:29:25.260
"We don't even have chats
about things anymore."

00:29:25.260 --> 00:29:27.690
Racial relations, indeed we don't.

00:29:27.690 --> 00:29:28.820
And guess what?

00:29:28.820 --> 00:29:31.300
You wanna be the first guy in?
- (laughs) Right, exactly.

00:29:31.300 --> 00:29:32.133
- Huh, really?

00:29:32.133 --> 00:29:33.990
You wanna be over this afternoon trying

00:29:33.990 --> 00:29:37.580
to make some sort of lucid nuanced comment

00:29:37.580 --> 00:29:41.300
about interactions between human
beings of different colors?

00:29:41.300 --> 00:29:42.870
You don't wanna be the first guy in.

00:29:42.870 --> 00:29:44.170
You're done.
- It's so funny,

00:29:44.170 --> 00:29:46.370
'cause people think it's something new,

00:29:46.370 --> 00:29:49.350
but you remember what
took out Howard Cosell

00:29:49.350 --> 00:29:51.825
40 years ago?
- Oh yeah, Alvin Garrett.

00:29:51.825 --> 00:29:52.920
- That's right, I think
there were two things.

00:29:52.920 --> 00:29:54.360
One was about his grandkids.

00:29:54.360 --> 00:29:55.700
Remember he, oh, oh, yeah right.

00:29:55.700 --> 00:29:56.533
He was the guy that he--
- Alvin Garrett.

00:29:56.533 --> 00:29:58.250
- And he called him a
little monkey or something--

00:29:58.250 --> 00:30:00.030
- Yeah, and number 46 for--
- Yeah.

00:30:00.030 --> 00:30:01.680
- And when you--
- And that's what

00:30:01.680 --> 00:30:03.810
he called his grandkids, just
'cause he was jumping around.

00:30:03.810 --> 00:30:05.950
He didn't mean it to be racist in any way.

00:30:05.950 --> 00:30:08.170
And no one felt he was racist
in any way before that.

00:30:08.170 --> 00:30:10.600
- Well, listen, when you,

00:30:10.600 --> 00:30:13.800
I think Cosell was just shy
of being an ACLU lawyer.

00:30:13.800 --> 00:30:16.690
I used to read Cosell in
"Sport Magazine" once a month,

00:30:16.690 --> 00:30:18.930
and he was always at the
vanguard of these movements.

00:30:18.930 --> 00:30:21.330
Who was tighter with Ali, for God sakes?

00:30:21.330 --> 00:30:24.060
And I saw a special on
Ali the other night,

00:30:24.060 --> 00:30:26.190
and it was called, "I Am Ali".

00:30:26.190 --> 00:30:27.860
I was reminded, I didn't not appreciate

00:30:27.860 --> 00:30:28.990
what he did to Joe Frazier.

00:30:28.990 --> 00:30:30.090
Indeed, he missed his own,

00:30:30.090 --> 00:30:31.760
he buried his own lead at some point

00:30:31.760 --> 00:30:34.140
when he called Frazier
names and stuff like that.

00:30:34.140 --> 00:30:35.557
And they have Marvis
Frazier I'm talking about,

00:30:35.557 --> 00:30:37.630
how it hurt his father
and hurt his family.

00:30:37.630 --> 00:30:39.110
So Ali wasn't perfect, either.

00:30:39.110 --> 00:30:42.200
But I watched a guy operate between races

00:30:42.200 --> 00:30:44.207
so seamlessly in that thing, I thought,

00:30:44.207 --> 00:30:46.610
"There's what we should
all be striving to get to".

00:30:46.610 --> 00:30:49.760
Not being complete, Ali was so beautiful.

00:30:49.760 --> 00:30:52.940
He was so proud of being
black and beautiful.

00:30:52.940 --> 00:30:55.370
And then he'd be with these
old square white people

00:30:55.370 --> 00:30:57.980
teasing with 'em, and
he's be with the grannies,

00:30:57.980 --> 00:31:00.087
then he'd be with the
children, and I thought,

00:31:00.087 --> 00:31:01.520
"Now, here's a guy who gets it."

00:31:01.520 --> 00:31:03.010
Gets the human condition.

00:31:03.010 --> 00:31:04.350
But we're nowhere near that.

00:31:04.350 --> 00:31:06.490
It's so uptight now it's crazy.

00:31:06.490 --> 00:31:08.520
- This is total offshoot.

00:31:08.520 --> 00:31:11.050
But the references.

00:31:11.050 --> 00:31:13.390
Is your brain just designed that way?

00:31:13.390 --> 00:31:16.660
- Well, listen, I realize my liabilities,

00:31:16.660 --> 00:31:19.440
and I would say one thing
that I would say I'm okay at

00:31:19.440 --> 00:31:23.070
is I have a reasonably
deep cultural drawer

00:31:23.070 --> 00:31:25.440
and a somewhat quick retrieval system.

00:31:25.440 --> 00:31:27.450
When I see those Christmas
stories every year

00:31:27.450 --> 00:31:29.280
where the kids are working

00:31:29.280 --> 00:31:31.180
in the Amazon disbursement centers

00:31:31.180 --> 00:31:34.180
in Minnetola or something
and they got packages coming

00:31:34.180 --> 00:31:35.710
at 'em at the speed of light,

00:31:35.710 --> 00:31:38.200
you asked me what I wanted
to do for a living earlier.

00:31:38.200 --> 00:31:40.920
I think I coulda stood at
the y in the road and said,

00:31:40.920 --> 00:31:44.730
okay, emery boards,
headphones, boom, boom, boom.

00:31:44.730 --> 00:31:46.210
I think I could have been the,

00:31:46.210 --> 00:31:48.060
what do they call it on the kitchen shows?

00:31:48.060 --> 00:31:51.210
I think I coulda worked the
pass on cultural referencing.

00:31:51.210 --> 00:31:54.010
- Yeah, so you just, but
that was just natural to you

00:31:54.010 --> 00:31:55.300
that it was just sort of all there?

00:31:55.300 --> 00:31:56.907
Or was that something
that you were going for?

00:31:56.907 --> 00:31:58.770
And you were like, oh
that'll be my shtick?

00:31:58.770 --> 00:32:01.410
- Well, I think it's a
bit of a monkey trick,

00:32:01.410 --> 00:32:03.810
so I don't know where you get that,

00:32:03.810 --> 00:32:05.830
nor do I think it's anything that you say

00:32:05.830 --> 00:32:07.800
where you get that, but I do remember

00:32:07.800 --> 00:32:10.850
on "Saturday Night Live" when I would get

00:32:10.850 --> 00:32:14.550
to write the news, and after a few weeks

00:32:14.550 --> 00:32:17.020
they had so many holes in
the dyke they let me kinda,

00:32:17.020 --> 00:32:19.400
me and Herb Sargent were writing the news.

00:32:19.400 --> 00:32:20.370
Other people would write jokes,

00:32:20.370 --> 00:32:22.340
but we predominately wrote it.

00:32:22.340 --> 00:32:25.020
And I noticed you have to
get actual about yourself.

00:32:25.020 --> 00:32:28.280
I couldn't, I needed adrenaline
to be really creative.

00:32:28.280 --> 00:32:30.200
And adrenaline came from fear for me.

00:32:30.200 --> 00:32:32.180
So I would notice I could not,

00:32:32.180 --> 00:32:34.140
occasionally a joke would pop into my head

00:32:34.140 --> 00:32:35.740
Monday through Thursday,

00:32:35.740 --> 00:32:38.510
but I could see life on the
griddle was what perked me.

00:32:38.510 --> 00:32:42.210
So Friday around 4:30 in the
afternoon, it was really funny,

00:32:42.210 --> 00:32:43.970
I'd think about Whitney Brown.

00:32:43.970 --> 00:32:45.680
And he was so smart.

00:32:45.680 --> 00:32:48.700
A little more malevolent
than me, but a brilliant cat.

00:32:48.700 --> 00:32:51.180
And we were kinda friends,
but kinda I always thought

00:32:51.180 --> 00:32:54.010
if we go to a two-anchor
thing it's Whitney.

00:32:54.010 --> 00:32:57.120
So 4:30 in the afternoon on
Friday, I think of Whitney.

00:32:57.120 --> 00:32:59.107
And I think, "Brother, you
better get this thing together,

00:32:59.107 --> 00:33:02.390
"you better quit this, I can't write."

00:33:02.390 --> 00:33:04.610
You do three bad ones of these in a row

00:33:04.610 --> 00:33:06.920
you're either out or you're co-hosting.

00:33:06.920 --> 00:33:08.030
And that's one thing I'm proud of.

00:33:08.030 --> 00:33:09.790
Made it six years and never co-hosted.

00:33:09.790 --> 00:33:12.590
So at 4:30.
- Now they all are, right?

00:33:12.590 --> 00:33:14.060
For years now, I think.

00:33:14.060 --> 00:33:15.260
- I don't know.
- I mean, sure.

00:33:15.260 --> 00:33:16.360
- There were guys who did it alone,

00:33:16.360 --> 00:33:20.127
but I always look back on that and think,

00:33:20.127 --> 00:33:21.043
"Well, that was a good thing."

00:33:21.043 --> 00:33:24.420
When you were served your
opportunity, you stepped up to it.

00:33:24.420 --> 00:33:27.180
But I remember I had a
big, three big sheets

00:33:27.180 --> 00:33:30.830
of paper about that size on
my wall, three different ones.

00:33:30.830 --> 00:33:32.990
And it would say "Indignation",

00:33:32.990 --> 00:33:35.310
and then the middle one said, "What am I?"

00:33:35.310 --> 00:33:38.574
And then the third one
said, "Arcane reference".

00:33:38.574 --> 00:33:39.946
(Dave laughing)
And it was like,

00:33:39.946 --> 00:33:41.440
it was like a haiku for "Update".

00:33:41.440 --> 00:33:44.100
I'd get to a point where
I'd think, oh, (murmuring)

00:33:44.100 --> 00:33:46.963
and I'd just look at that
and go, what pisses you off?

00:33:48.070 --> 00:33:50.220
Put the see saw in, the fulcrum.

00:33:50.220 --> 00:33:51.053
What am I?

00:33:52.430 --> 00:33:54.030
The Jetsons' robot maid.

00:33:54.030 --> 00:33:58.400
I used to, like, make
it reductio absurdum.

00:33:58.400 --> 00:34:00.110
I really got primal at that point.

00:34:00.110 --> 00:34:01.473
I felt like I was, like,

00:34:03.110 --> 00:34:04.483
like you're a great white.

00:34:05.405 --> 00:34:07.520
You might as well heard
the theme from "Jaws"

00:34:07.520 --> 00:34:09.710
as you're just swimming
looking for the line.

00:34:09.710 --> 00:34:12.480
'Cause I got scared that
I would lose the gig.

00:34:12.480 --> 00:34:14.370
But I did have that template in my head.

00:34:14.370 --> 00:34:15.680
So maybe that's where the references--

00:34:15.680 --> 00:34:16.850
- Yeah, and now it just seems like it's

00:34:16.850 --> 00:34:18.040
just sort of built in or something.

00:34:18.040 --> 00:34:20.340
Like, it just became.
- Well, I must say that

00:34:21.440 --> 00:34:23.900
even before I became a comedian,

00:34:23.900 --> 00:34:25.675
goofy stuff would stick in my head.

00:34:25.675 --> 00:34:26.508
I don't know.
- Yeah.

00:34:26.508 --> 00:34:27.430
But I remember watching one of your,

00:34:27.430 --> 00:34:30.280
how many HBO specials have you done?

00:34:30.280 --> 00:34:32.930
- Well, I've got nine one-hour specials.

00:34:32.930 --> 00:34:35.000
Eight of them were HBO.
- Eight on HBO.

00:34:35.000 --> 00:34:36.510
So I'm thinking it must have been

00:34:36.510 --> 00:34:38.210
one of your first ones
I remember watching.

00:34:38.210 --> 00:34:39.043
Again, I'm young.

00:34:39.043 --> 00:34:40.727
I'm 15, something like that, thinking,

00:34:40.727 --> 00:34:42.750
"I don't get all of this, but I like it."

00:34:42.750 --> 00:34:43.583
Do you know what I mean?

00:34:43.583 --> 00:34:44.730
Like, there were things
that you were saying

00:34:44.730 --> 00:34:47.057
that I was like, "I don't
know what that reference is,

00:34:47.057 --> 00:34:48.307
"but I kinda liked it", 'cause I was like,

00:34:48.307 --> 00:34:49.377
"other people are laughing,

00:34:49.377 --> 00:34:50.740
"so there must be something here."

00:34:50.740 --> 00:34:52.567
- It's not the Warren Commission, brother.

00:34:52.567 --> 00:34:53.730
(Dave laughing)
At some point,

00:34:53.730 --> 00:34:56.169
the rhythms of it are intoxicating.

00:34:56.169 --> 00:34:58.269
I just remember thinking, "Lean into this.

00:34:59.677 --> 00:35:01.487
"If you're gonna rat a tat tat,

00:35:01.487 --> 00:35:04.410
"you're working that speed
bag, you can't miss it."

00:35:04.410 --> 00:35:07.180
I got the words down, I delivered them,

00:35:07.180 --> 00:35:09.567
and I remember thinking,
"If they don't get

00:35:09.567 --> 00:35:14.567
"the actual content of this,
do the d'Artagnan thing.

00:35:15.517 --> 00:35:16.730
"Flourish."

00:35:16.730 --> 00:35:17.609
You know?
- Yeah.

00:35:17.609 --> 00:35:18.442
- Boom.

00:35:18.442 --> 00:35:20.810
You don't always have to prick
the other guy's chest plate.

00:35:20.810 --> 00:35:23.307
But you at least gotta stand out there

00:35:23.307 --> 00:35:25.290
and have the blade twang.

00:35:25.290 --> 00:35:27.830
I was always making sure
the right blade twanged.

00:35:27.830 --> 00:35:29.720
Ba-boom!
- God, that's so funny

00:35:29.720 --> 00:35:31.100
to me, to now, I'd like to go back

00:35:31.100 --> 00:35:32.280
and watch some of them now

00:35:32.280 --> 00:35:33.420
and sort of unpack a little of that.

00:35:33.420 --> 00:35:34.960
- Yeah, you can't half stick that.

00:35:34.960 --> 00:35:35.880
- Yeah.
- And I always knew

00:35:35.880 --> 00:35:37.900
it was a bit of a scam.
- Yeah.

00:35:37.900 --> 00:35:40.730
- Listen, I'm not, what I have

00:35:40.730 --> 00:35:42.360
on my board's like 10, 20.

00:35:42.360 --> 00:35:45.920
I'm not, like I said,
I could remember things

00:35:45.920 --> 00:35:47.140
and get to them quickly.

00:35:47.140 --> 00:35:48.757
And then I was smart enough to think,

00:35:48.757 --> 00:35:50.667
"You can't half ass this.

00:35:50.667 --> 00:35:52.890
"Sheepish is the death of this."

00:35:52.890 --> 00:35:54.440
You've gotta lean into it.

00:35:54.440 --> 00:35:57.050
And then I used to always
love Carson's moves when he--

00:35:57.050 --> 00:35:59.300
- Yeah, yeah.
- Johnny Blue One,

00:35:59.300 --> 00:36:01.060
or it wasn't there.

00:36:01.060 --> 00:36:02.423
I used to find that intoxicating.

00:36:02.423 --> 00:36:04.860
I would think, "Look how debonair"--

00:36:04.860 --> 00:36:05.882
- Just the little head movement, little-

00:36:05.882 --> 00:36:06.715
- Smooth.

00:36:08.780 --> 00:36:10.947
I used to sit at home, I didn't
even know what showbiz was,

00:36:10.947 --> 00:36:13.560
and I'd think, "Now, there's a cool cat."

00:36:13.560 --> 00:36:17.440
It's in the moments where you
need to escape the bad one.

00:36:17.440 --> 00:36:20.090
So that was another thing
I did on "Weekend Update".

00:36:20.090 --> 00:36:22.337
I always remember thinking,
"Some of these are dogs.

00:36:22.337 --> 00:36:25.387
"When it dies, a dog's death,
you gotta be alert enough

00:36:25.387 --> 00:36:26.637
"to say that in front of the people,

00:36:26.637 --> 00:36:28.610
"they'll find that intoxicating."

00:36:28.610 --> 00:36:29.740
Like, you gotta look up and say,

00:36:29.740 --> 00:36:32.380
no, I'm not so needy that my self esteem's

00:36:32.380 --> 00:36:33.870
in your hands right now.

00:36:33.870 --> 00:36:35.510
But I, too, know that was a bad joke.

00:36:35.510 --> 00:36:37.210
- Yeah, but a lot of guys since you

00:36:37.210 --> 00:36:38.610
have fallen into that crack.

00:36:39.560 --> 00:36:40.393
- Well, what do you mean?

00:36:40.393 --> 00:36:41.226
Tell me.

00:36:41.226 --> 00:36:42.059
Do you mean guys looking
for applause and stuff?

00:36:42.059 --> 00:36:43.330
Yeah.
- Yeah, when they know

00:36:43.330 --> 00:36:45.266
it's not working, where
you would shake it,

00:36:45.266 --> 00:36:46.099
I mean you would
literally, you just did it.

00:36:46.099 --> 00:36:48.000
But you would sorta shake it off, kinda,

00:36:48.000 --> 00:36:51.200
where they're kinda like, ah, please.

00:36:51.200 --> 00:36:52.820
- Well--
- It's just the nature of it.

00:36:52.820 --> 00:36:54.480
- I don't watch enough young,

00:36:54.480 --> 00:36:55.860
there's a few cats I watch.

00:36:55.860 --> 00:36:58.040
I think Maniscalco's like a stone.

00:36:58.040 --> 00:37:00.400
Killer, I love his rhythms.

00:37:00.400 --> 00:37:03.300
And Brian's a great,
Reagan's a great comedian.

00:37:03.300 --> 00:37:04.880
But I don't watch enough
of the young guys.

00:37:04.880 --> 00:37:06.470
I do know that at some point,

00:37:06.470 --> 00:37:10.180
comedy almost turned into what
impressionists used to get.

00:37:10.180 --> 00:37:11.650
Where you get applause.

00:37:11.650 --> 00:37:14.200
I almost feel like, instead of you going,

00:37:14.200 --> 00:37:16.490
Al Pacino working in a Burger King,

00:37:16.490 --> 00:37:18.663
I think it would go something like this.

00:37:18.663 --> 00:37:19.790
(Dave laughing)
I feel like guys

00:37:19.790 --> 00:37:21.770
are doing that with their
political opinions now.

00:37:21.770 --> 00:37:24.190
I think, and then they present it.

00:37:24.190 --> 00:37:25.607
And you don't hear anybody laughing,

00:37:25.607 --> 00:37:26.730
you just hear this.
(Dennis applauding)

00:37:26.730 --> 00:37:28.550
And I was never into that.
- Yeah.

00:37:28.550 --> 00:37:31.150
So even though I can sense
that Dennis Miller 2020

00:37:31.150 --> 00:37:33.260
is slightly evolved out of, maybe,

00:37:33.260 --> 00:37:36.720
the full grind and fight thing,

00:37:36.720 --> 00:37:39.030
where are you sort of at, politically?

00:37:39.030 --> 00:37:40.410
I asked you right before we started,

00:37:40.410 --> 00:37:42.940
and it's like, you strike
as you're probably, mostly--

00:37:42.940 --> 00:37:44.500
- I'm socially liberal.
- Yeah, you strike me

00:37:44.500 --> 00:37:46.513
as mostly libertarian
kinda something like that.

00:37:46.513 --> 00:37:47.910
- I come in, I meet your husband.

00:37:47.910 --> 00:37:49.570
I can't believe there
are people in the world

00:37:49.570 --> 00:37:52.560
who don't see people in love
and just be happy for 'em.

00:37:52.560 --> 00:37:55.000
I see the picture of all these,

00:37:55.000 --> 00:37:56.140
I don't wanna talk about your life--

00:37:56.140 --> 00:37:57.140
- No you can.
- Your beautiful,

00:37:57.140 --> 00:38:00.980
big family Walton Mountain
shot, everybody's smiling,

00:38:00.980 --> 00:38:02.700
you two guys are at the center of it.

00:38:02.700 --> 00:38:03.870
I don't know if it's your wedding day

00:38:03.870 --> 00:38:05.460
or whatever that is.
- Yeah, it's wedding day.

00:38:05.460 --> 00:38:07.687
- And I'm thinking, "How do
you get to the point in life

00:38:07.687 --> 00:38:09.917
"where you're going up
to strangers and saying,

00:38:09.917 --> 00:38:11.567
"here's what I think about that."

00:38:12.530 --> 00:38:14.270
(Dave laughing)
That amazes me

00:38:14.270 --> 00:38:18.330
that there's that, so
that part of Conservatism.

00:38:18.330 --> 00:38:19.980
And I'm not putting
them, I know there's some

00:38:19.980 --> 00:38:23.200
Christian people who think they
go to hell if they sanction.

00:38:23.200 --> 00:38:25.040
I'm not gonna get, I'm not their shrink,

00:38:25.040 --> 00:38:27.590
I'm can't get into their
life and figure all that out.

00:38:27.590 --> 00:38:29.020
I just know I'm a believer,

00:38:29.020 --> 00:38:32.620
and I've never had a chat with
my God about homosexuality.

00:38:32.620 --> 00:38:34.519
It just, it didn't--
- It never came up.

00:38:34.519 --> 00:38:35.740
- In my thing.
- It never came up, huh?

00:38:35.740 --> 00:38:38.770
- And I met enough fervent
believers who are homosexual,

00:38:38.770 --> 00:38:41.007
I think, "What, does God say
I can't talk to you today?

00:38:41.007 --> 00:38:41.840
"You're gay."

00:38:41.840 --> 00:38:43.930
So that whole thing doesn't exist for me.

00:38:43.930 --> 00:38:46.730
I don't trust radical Islam
as far as I can throw it.

00:38:46.730 --> 00:38:48.160
I'd like to keep half my money.

00:38:48.160 --> 00:38:49.940
Some people think that's
a piggish statement,

00:38:49.940 --> 00:38:51.670
but I always think--
- Boy, you greedy bastard.

00:38:51.670 --> 00:38:54.080
- Yeah, but I think, "I'll keep one."

00:38:54.080 --> 00:38:56.549
I give you one, I don't even know you.

00:38:56.549 --> 00:38:59.390
And then people say, and
after I pay taxes on it,

00:38:59.390 --> 00:39:00.840
I'd like to keep it for my kids.

00:39:00.840 --> 00:39:03.210
And then they go, why do
you wanna spoil your kids

00:39:03.210 --> 00:39:05.290
with an inheritance?

